## Plan pipeline

This plan ticket has an `Artifacts wanted` section, so its plan run builds the
artifacts it checks before the split, in the order real product work follows:
understand the domain, map the journeys, class the features, show them as
screens, record the decisions, then split the work. Every plan run on it (a new
plan, a resume, a revision or a re-plan) follows this section together with
`Parent tickets`; step 1 of the plan run there, writing the planned split, comes
after the stages below.

### Which stages to produce

- Produce a stage only when its box is checked under `Artifacts wanted`
  (`[x]`). Skip every unchecked stage: no document, no placeholder.
- The implementation plan (stage 6) is always produced, whatever is checked.
  With no box checked, the run is today's plain split into sub-tickets.
- A stage builds on the stages before it that were produced. When an earlier
  stage it needs is unchecked, take what it needs from the description and say
  so at the top of its document.

### The stages, in order

Each stage produces one Linear document, created with
`linear_create_document(title, content)` under the title given here (Symphony
prefixes the identifier). On a later run, find the existing one with
`linear_get_document` and edit it with `linear_update_document`; never create a
second document for a stage.

1. **Domain brief** (document `Domain brief`): the domain, the people in it, its
   vocabulary (a glossary), its constraints (rules, regulation, physical or
   social facts the product must respect) and the sources you drew on.
2. **User journeys** (document `User journeys`): the personas and the jobs each
   one hires the product for, then every journey end to end, step by step. Cover
   the routine journeys every product has, not only the niche ones: first launch,
   everyday use, fixing a mistake, finding something again, changing a setting,
   and moving to a new phone.
3. **Kano feature map** (document `Kano feature map`): every feature the journeys
   need, in a table with its Kano class (Must-be, Performance, Attractive or
   Indifferent), the journeys it serves and why it has that class. Always add the
   platform baseline from the house standards as Must-be, whatever the niche.
4. **Screens** (document `Screens`, plus one HTML file):
   - The document lays out each journey screen by screen. For each screen: what
     it shows, its states (empty, loading, filled, error, and any other the
     journey reaches), the actions on it and where each leads, and the platform
     conventions it follows: Material 3 on Android, the Human Interface
     Guidelines on Apple platforms. Add an image per screen where one can be
     rendered offscreen.
   - **One self-contained HTML file** shows every screen in the document, each
     labelled with its journey and screen name, styled for the platform, with no
     external assets (inline CSS and SVG only: no web fonts, CDNs, scripts from
     elsewhere or remote images), so it opens in a desktop browser offline.
     Write it inside the workspace, at `plan-artifacts/screens.html` (never
     under `$TMPDIR`: `linear_attach_file` refuses a file outside the workspace;
     a plan run never commits, so the folder stays untracked), attach it to the
     ticket with `linear_attach_file`, and link it at the top of the Screens
     document. On a later run, attach the revised file and replace the link;
     this file is the one attachment a plan run makes.
5. **Decisions** (document `Decisions`): one ADR-style section per real choice:
   the context, the options, the one you recommend and why, and what it costs.
   Only real choices: a question with one sensible answer is not a decision.
6. **Implementation plan**: the sub-tickets, filed as in the plan run of
   `Parent tickets` (one PR each, `blocked_by` links, the `Final verification:`
   sub-ticket last). Every sub-ticket names the feature it builds, the journey
   and the screens it serves and the feature's Kano class (feature → journey →
   screen → ticket), and carries a `## User walkthrough`: numbered steps a user
   takes, each with the result they should see. File the Must-be tickets first,
   then Performance, then Attractive; Indifferent features get no ticket unless
   the description asks for them. With no Kano map, name the part of the
   description each sub-ticket serves instead.

### House standards

Load the house standards on every run that produces a Kano map or screens: read
`docs/standards/house-standards.md` in this workspace. A project overrides it
with a Linear document named `House standards`: when the description or a
comment links or quotes one, follow it instead and say so in the Kano map. With
neither, use this baseline, every item Must-be: edit, delete, undo, search,
backup/export/restore, settings, accessibility, offline use, notification
control and privacy controls. The design quality bar in the standards binds the
screens and the HTML file.

### Rubric per stage

Before handing over, check every artifact you produced against its rubric, fix
what fails, and record the check in the workpad `Notes`, one line per rubric.

- **Domain brief:** names the people, has a glossary, lists the constraints and
  cites at least one source; nothing in it is a feature or a screen yet.
- **User journeys:** every persona has at least one journey; every journey runs
  end to end; first launch, fixing a mistake and moving to a new phone are
  covered.
- **Kano feature map:** every feature from the journeys is in it with one class;
  every platform baseline item from the house standards is in it as Must-be;
  every feature names a journey it serves.
- **Screens:** every Must-be feature has a screen; every journey step that shows
  something has a screen; every screen lists its states and its platform
  conventions; the HTML file shows every screen in the document and no other,
  loads nothing from outside the file, and is linked at the top of the document.
- **Decisions:** every decision has at least two options and one recommendation
  with its reason; every open question of the run is here.
- **Implementation plan:** every sub-ticket traces to a feature, a journey and a
  Kano class; every Must-be feature is covered by a sub-ticket; Must-be tickets
  come first; every sub-ticket has acceptance criteria and a
  `## User walkthrough`; the `Final verification:` sub-ticket is blocked by every
  other one and lists their criteria.

### One review, at the end

- Produce every wanted stage, then the sub-tickets, then hand over once. Never
  stop between stages for review, never move the ticket after one stage, and
  never wait for a stage to be approved: a later stage builds on the earlier one
  as written.
- An open question never stops the run: write it as a decision with the options
  and a recommended default, go on with the default, and list it under
  `Decisions needed` in the review brief.
- The review brief (see `Review brief`) links every document, the HTML screens
  and the `Decisions` document, lists every sub-ticket and every decision needed.
  The summary block's `artifacts` link every document and the HTML screens next
  to the sub-tickets.
- A comment on any part brings a plan revision run (see `Parent tickets`): it
  edits the documents with `linear_update_document`, attaches a revised HTML file
  and relinks it, and brings the `Backlog` sub-tickets in line, all in place. A
  change to an early stage flows through the later ones: dropping a journey
  drops its features, screens and sub-tickets. Then it reruns the rubrics and
  updates the brief.
